    {
      "title": "Context compaction preserves intent while dropping essential operational details",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://vshulcz.github.io/deja-vu/guide/after-compaction.html",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "vshulcz"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn why LLM session compaction systematically drops critical low-level details like exact commands, numbers, and file paths while preserving prose summaries. This analysis quantifies what is lost during compression and explains why high-level summaries fail during technical debugging.",
      "hn_id": "50627573",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50627573",
      "image": "/infographics/01-hn-50627573.jpg",
      "content": "Context compaction in coding agents creates a silent failure mode that catches many engineers off guard. An empirical study analyzing 2.59 gigabytes of conversation transcripts across 43 compaction events uncovered a massive structural bias in what gets preserved.\n\nWhile summaries retained roughly 77 percent of prose decisions and rationales, they kept only 9.8 percent of file paths and a staggering 0.2 percent of executed shell commands. Measured numbers and specific error messages suffered an identical fate, dropping almost entirely out of context.\n\nThis explains why a coding agent appears to remember architectural intent yet suddenly forgets the exact flags, connection pool parameters, or test invocations from twenty minutes prior. Compaction algorithms naturally optimize for conceptual narrative at the expense of technical state.\n\nIf you build agent harnesses or context windows, relying solely on recursive summarization will degrade tool precision. Production harnesses require dedicated secondary stores to persist raw terminal interactions, environment diffs, and exact error payloads alongside conversational summaries.\n\nContext management cannot treat raw execution logs and high-level reasoning as the same data type."
    },
    {
      "title": "Designing an inverted index for massive agent traces",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.langchain.com/blog/full-text-search-in-smithdb-designing-an-inverted-index-for-object-storage",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Ankush Gola",
        "Akshay Aurora",
        "Sumedh Arani"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how to design an inverted index tailored for massive, deeply nested agent traces on object storage. You will learn how indexing economics shift when documents routinely span megabytes of context.",
      "hn_id": "50636389",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50636389",
      "image": "/infographics/02-hn-50636389.jpg",
      "content": "Traditional search engines like Lucene or Tantivy assume an inverted index built over billions of small documents. When LangChain built SmithDB to index production AI agent traces, that assumption completely broke down.\n\nAgent traces feature massive, deeply nested JSON blobs where input and output context payloads regularly exceed several megabytes. Traditional approaches cause posting lists to explode in size while degrading object storage throughput. SmithDB redesigned inverted indexing around these skewed document distributions, optimizing data layouts on object storage to hit 400 millisecond median query latencies across massive traces.\n\nHandling huge payloads in object storage requires tight alignment between column chunking, dictionary compression, and selective posting list materialization. By decoupling metadata filtering from full-text inverted lookups and skipping large text payload retrieval until post-filtering, the engine avoids saturating network bandwidth on cloud storage.\n\nIf you are designing log aggregation or observability systems for long-running LLM workloads, building on top of standard search defaults will quickly hit severe economic and latency limits."
    },
    {
      "title": "Agents cannot compromise resources absent from their namespace",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://infernode.io/",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "lproven"
      ],
      "why_read": "Discover how capability-based namespace containment isolates AI agents by exposing only explicitly granted resources. You will understand how operating-system-level boundaries offer stronger security than reliance on model guardrails.",
      "hn_id": "50632533",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632533",
      "image": "/infographics/03-hn-50632533.jpg",
      "content": "Current agent sandboxing strategies present a flawed compromise: engineers either execute agents locally and trust model guardrails, or hand execution over to remote microVM platforms that introduce heavy operational latency. InferNode approaches this constraint through capability-based operating system design.\n\nBy modernizing Bell Labs' Inferno OS into a 64-bit runtime, InferNode enforces strict namespace containment. An agent operates inside a custom namespace containing solely the resources explicitly mounted for its task. If an SSH key or credential environment variable is not provisioned into the agent view, it does not exist in its memory space, completely neutralizing prompt injection attempts aimed at credential discovery.\n\nThe runtime achieves a 15 to 30 MB memory footprint with cold start times under 2 seconds, which is dramatically lighter than typical Linux container or microVM alternatives. Furthermore, the isolation primitives are formally model-checked using TLA+, SPIN, and CBMC, while write activity gets committed to an external hash-chained audit log that the agent cannot tamper with.\n\nTrue agent containment belongs at the capability and namespace level rather than prompt-level guardrails."
    },
    {
      "title": "Seabraid lets heterogeneous GPUs serve one mixture of experts layer",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/saltwater-research/seabraid",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "saltwater-research"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand how Seabraid orchestrates cross-vendor GPU execution to cooperatively serve mixture-of-experts layers using native vendor kernels.",
      "hn_id": "50625445",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50625445",
      "image": "/infographics/04-github-50625445.jpg",
      "content": "Serving Mixture-of-Experts models typically locks infrastructure teams into homogeneous hardware pools. If you have surplus AMD ROCm cards alongside an Nvidia cluster, combining them across a single model layer has historically introduced crippling synchronization and driver incompatibilities.\n\nSeabraid solves this by operating as an engine-neutral runtime beneath the inference engine. The host engine retains its global scheduler, KV cache, and weight loader, but delegates the routed token rows directly to Seabraid. From there, Seabraid partitions expert placement across CUDA and ROCm devices simultaneously, dispatching computations to vendor-native kernels through explicit session and ticket abstraction layers.\n\nThis architecture bypasses the need for homogeneous clusters during large MoE deployments. Instead of stranding compute capacity on mismatched accelerator generations or competing vendors, you can braid disparate compute pools directly at the expert routing layer.\n\nHeterogeneous hardware scheduling is rapidly shifting from academic curiosity to mandatory infrastructure practice."
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch suffers widespread numerical errors when computing KL divergence",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30835",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Zhen Yang",
        "Wei Tang",
        "Chu Chen",
        "Rui Qian",
        "Yixuan Chen",
        "Fan Yang",
        "Kangning Cui"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the root causes of catastrophic cancellation in standard KL divergence implementations across deep learning frameworks. You will learn how floating-point instability silently corrupts model distillation and circuit analysis, along with practical mathematical repairs to fix it.",
      "hn_id": "50629962",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50629962",
      "image": "/infographics/05-hn-50629962.jpg",
      "content": "The standard PyTorch implementation of Kullback-Leibler divergence fails silently on nearby distributions, generating catastrophic numerical errors and impossible negative values.\n\nWhen evaluating 8.5 million inputs across 18 closed-form distribution families, researchers discovered that over 85 percent of 32-bit floating-point calculations suffer relative errors exceeding one percent near small divergence thresholds. Simply increasing precision to 64-bit floating point fails to resolve the issue, leaving up to 51 percent of configurations significantly inaccurate due to severe floating-point cancellation.\n\nThese inaccuracies directly corrupt machine learning workflows. Identical-start model distillation exhibits up to 97 percent top-one disagreement, while transformer circuit edge evaluations and quantized layer rankings shift unpredictably under default loss formulations.\n\nReplacing standard formulas with stable closed-form decompositions, compensated summation, and analytical gradients eliminates 97 percent of these errors without altering runtime performance.\n\nDo not clamp negative divergence values to zero when proper numerical formulation fixes the underlying math."
    },
    {
      "title": "Compiling web interfaces into verified operations stabilizes AI agents",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://protocolis.co/paper/",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "vahagnb"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand how compiling live web interfaces into verified operations enables AI agents to drastically boost task completion rates while slashing inference costs. You will learn a mechanistic alternative to brittle browser tool calling without relying on site-specific adapters.",
      "hn_id": "50632998",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632998",
      "image": "/infographics/06-hn-50632998.jpg",
      "content": "Web-navigating AI agents frequently fail because they treat dynamic web interfaces as raw perceptual input rather than executable state machines.\n\nProtocolis introduces solid-state web execution, which compiles live web pages into verified, discrete operations without making continuous model calls or requiring custom site adapters. In extensive benchmarks over thousands of runs, this execution model completed 76 percent of long-horizon tasks compared to only 46 percent for standard agent implementations.\n\nBecause the compiler derives controls directly from the interface and verifies effects automatically, it cuts token costs dramatically. A small model running on this architecture outperformed frontier models on alternative frameworks in 24 out of 30 test configurations while costing up to 26 times less per task.\n\nSenior engineers building agentic workflows should take note: moving verification and interface parsing out of the model prompt and into a deterministic compiler layer eliminates the primary source of agent drift.\n\nDeterministic interface compilation beats prompt engineering for reliable web automation."
    },
    {
      "title": "Formal verification in Lean reveals hidden bugs in Clojure",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://s.conao3.com/blog/2026/e4f733",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Conao3"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how combining Lean formal verification with differential testing uncovered latent bugs in Clojure's core collections and Java interop layer. It provides a practical demonstration of modeling real-world language runtimes to catch edge-case bugs.",
      "hn_id": "50629523",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50629523",
      "image": "/infographics/07-hn-50629523.jpg",
      "content": "Even mature persistent data structures battle tested over fifteen years contain subtle edge-case bugs. A recent deep dive into the Clojure core runtime using Lean 4 and differential testing uncovered seventeen previously unreported bugs across immutable collections, specifically inside hash array mapped tries (HAMT) and primitive vectors.\n\nThe verification strategy picked apart the Java implementation by translating integer and bit arithmetic into Lean definitions. By modeling primitive operations with exact bit-width semantics, a counterexample solver could systematically identify overflow and wrap-around defects that millions of random test runs missed.\n\nMost defects did not hide inside the main tree traversal logic. Instead, they lingered at the seams where distinct mechanisms interact, such as hashing equality contracts and hash collision nodes. Differential testing executed over three million operations against standard collections without failing, yet formal property checks pinpointed exact boundary failures instantly.\n\nFormal methods are no longer purely academic exercises. Incorporating theorem provers like Lean into critical storage and collection routines is rapidly becoming a practical, highly effective verification pattern for systems engineers."
    },
    {
      "title": "Type errors cost coding agents under one percent of time",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://evilmartians.com/chronicles/type-errors-cost-our-coding-agents-under-one-percent-of-their-time",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Irina Nazarova"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to explore empirical data challenging the assumption that static type systems improve AI coding agent performance. You will learn how agents spend less than one percent of their time resolving type errors and perform equally well on dynamic languages.",
      "hn_id": "50632999",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632999",
      "image": "/infographics/08-hn-50632999.jpg",
      "content": "A widespread assumption in AI development is that static typing makes coding agents dramatically better because the compiler catches errors immediately. Empirical benchmarks across ten programming languages tell a very different story.\n\nEvil Martians evaluated real-world repository issues across languages like TypeScript, Python, Ruby, Go, and Rust. The outcome was a dead heat: both typed and dynamic languages achieved solve rates of roughly 65 percent. Even more surprising, agents invoked the type checker in only 1 out of 10 TypeScript runs and edited around language server diagnostics.\n\nType errors accounted for less than 1 percent of overall agent execution time. Instead, execution bottlenecks stemmed from slow browser verification tasks and fuzzy domain requirements. An agent spent 13 minutes running a single browser check, consuming more runtime than all type issues combined across twelve full Rails tasks.\n\nIf you are optimizing harness infrastructure for coding agents, static types are not the silver bullet many claimed. Focus instead on fast feedback loops and tighter execution environments."
    },
    {
      "title": "Database compression achieves high ratios with fast random access",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.baseshift.com/blog/dc1",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Amir More"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how DC1 delivers high compression ratios on unmodified PostgreSQL while preserving fast random access to eight-kilobyte blocks.",
      "hn_id": "50633015",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50633015",
      "image": "/infographics/09-hn-50633015.jpg",
      "content": "Database storage engines constantly juggle an awkward trade-off: larger compression blocks yield better compression ratios, but they destroy random read latency because every query must decompress surplus data.\n\nBaseshift introduced Database Compression V1 (DC1), a mechanism designed to bypass this limit on unmodified PostgreSQL binaries. Rather than modifying database internals or relying on specialized filesystems, they intercept I/O using a tailored libc. This architecture achieves 8.4x compression on the 242 GB OpenStreetMap dataset, shrinking it down to 28.7 GB while maintaining discrete 8 KB block random access.\n\nCrucially, decoding an 8 KB block requires an average of 0.18 milliseconds on a single modern CPU core. That decode latency is faster than round-trip requests to cloud block storage volumes like AWS EBS, making deep compression practical without crippling transactional reads.\n\nInterception at the libc boundary proves you do not always need custom storage engines or kernel drivers to solve persistent database I/O bottlenecks."
    },
    {
      "title": "Branching separates compute from storage to restore Postgres instantly",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://pgcraftsman.io/posts/restoring-5-tb-of-postgres-in-5-seconds/",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "PgCraftsman"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand how separating compute from storage enables copy-on-write branching for instantaneous database recovery. You will learn the mechanics behind how shared write-ahead log history allows multi-terabyte instances to clone in seconds.",
      "hn_id": "50636459",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50636459",
      "image": "/infographics/10-hn-50636459.jpg",
      "content": "Restoring a five-terabyte database traditionally means waiting hours for physical block copies or replaying massive transaction logs. Modern decoupled storage architectures change this dynamic entirely by making clones instantaneous through copy-on-write page trees.\n\nInstead of copying gigabytes of table heaps, a new database branch simply points to a specific log sequence number (LSN) in the parent WAL timeline. Both parent and child run on isolated compute nodes while reading identical page versions from immutable shared storage history.\n\nPostgres stores catalog schemas directly alongside user data in the buffer pool. Because of this design choice, pointing to an exact WAL LSN instantly mirrors the entire relational schema, table structures, and indexing states without moving bytes over the wire. Modified pages branch out on write, leaving original storage intact.\n\nDecoupling compute from storage transforms recovery from an operational bottleneck into a standard primitive for CI sandboxes and fast rollbacks."
    },
    {
      "title": "MCP servers can now ship skills alongside their tools",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://claude.com/resources/articles/skills-over-mcp-tasks-and-more",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "saikatsg"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how Model Context Protocol updates allow servers to provide actionable skills and tasks alongside tools. You will learn how modular guidance helps agents handle complex workflows while keeping context usage low.",
      "hn_id": "50632503",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632503",
      "image": "/infographics/11-hn-50632503.jpg",
      "content": "Model Context Protocol is shifting from basic remote tool execution to full agentic orchestration. Anthropic just introduced Skills over MCP, allowing servers to expose step-by-step guidance alongside tool schemas.\n\nHistorically, MCP servers described what functions an agent could call, but gave zero runtime context on multi-step workflows. To execute complex operations, models either needed bloated system prompts or trial-and-error reasoning loops. With Skills over MCP, servers publish targeted skills that load dynamically into context only when needed, minimizing token bloat while bundling operational playbooks right at the data source.\n\nAnthropic also released Tasks and Elicitation into general availability. This allows MCP servers to handle longer interactive work, pause execution to ask the model for missing inputs, and stream execution state back and forth cleanly.\n\nAgent infrastructure is rapidly converging on structured protocol design instead of brittle prompting layers."
    },
    {
      "title": "Coding agents build inference engines from scratch beating vllm",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/humanityslastsystem/hls",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Steven Gao",
        "Rulin Shao",
        "Keisuke Kamahori",
        "Yuetai Li",
        "Yufei Gao",
        "Atindra Jha",
        "Junjie Oscar Yin",
