        "Pang Wei Koh",
        "Luke Zettlemoyer",
        "Stephanie Wang",
        "Baris Kasikci"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how autonomous coding agents tackle complex, long-horizon systems engineering challenges. You will learn how agents can engineer specialized inference stacks from scratch that outperform production baselines.",
      "hn_id": "50632206",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50632206",
      "image": "/infographics/12-github-50632206.jpg",
      "content": "Benchmarking autonomous software engineers has long suffered from toy problems and contaminated unit test suites. Humanity is testing agents on an entirely different scale: asking them to write custom GPU inference engines from scratch in twelve hours to outperform vLLM.\n\nThe benchmark gives an agent an isolated environment with up to eight H200 GPUs and a target workload. Instead of patching a function, the agent must implement the compute kernels, memory pooling, and batching logic tailored to a single architecture. In reported tests, Opus 5 successfully beat production baselines in 71 percent of runs, achieving up to 25.9x speedups on speech models.\n\nThis shifts how we evaluate coding agents from syntax generation to end-to-end systems engineering. Writing performant CUDA memory management and scheduling requires genuine algorithmic reasoning across thousands of lines of code.\n\nDomain-specific inference systems designed on demand by autonomous agents may soon outperform generalized serving frameworks."
    },
    {
      "title": "Postgres extension combines hybrid search and in-place vector migration",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/univec-ai/postvec/",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "amorroxic"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how Postvec unifies semantic and BM25 search directly inside PostgreSQL. It demonstrates how to keep vectors synchronized automatically and migrate embedding models without re-embedding source text.",
      "hn_id": "50627889",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50627889",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Migrating between embedding models in a production vector database usually requires an expensive and slow re-computation of every single raw text record.\n\nPostvec takes an unconventional shortcut by implementing direct vector space migration inside PostgreSQL. Rather than pulling raw text back into memory and invoking an embedding model across millions of rows, it projects existing vector embeddings directly into the target model space through trained vector transformations.\n\nThe extension also embeds hybrid search directly into the database engine. It merges BM25 lexical ranking with semantic vector distance inside a single SQL query, keeping vector indexes synchronized with text updates automatically without background workers.\n\nInference can run locally inside the PostgreSQL process via embedded models or offload to external endpoints. This architecture drastically reduces operational complexity by removing the separate retrieval synchronization pipeline.\n\nEliminating the need to store and re-index raw source text turns embedding model upgrades from a multi-week migration into an in-place database transaction."
    },
    {
      "title": "Binary fuse filters save more space than Bloom filters",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://antonz.org/probabilistic-data-structures-2/",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Anton Zhiyanov"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how binary fuse filters improve on Bloom filters by trading mutability for lower memory consumption in read-heavy workloads.",
      "hn_id": "50636272",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50636272",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Standard Bloom filters are no longer the most space-efficient choice for immutable database files. When data is written once and read millions of times, such as in an LSM-tree SSTable or a bad-domain blocklist, binary fuse filters offer a significant leap forward in storage density.\n\nA binary fuse filter provides the exact same false-positive guarantees as a classic Bloom filter but reduces memory consumption by roughly twenty-five percent. For a dataset of one million items at a 0.39 percent false-positive rate, a fuse filter requires only nine bits per key compared to twelve bits for a tuned Bloom filter.\n\nThe core architectural trade-off is mutability. Binary fuse filters require the entire key set upfront during construction, which makes subsequent inserts impossible without rebuilding the structure from scratch.\n\nFor storage engines that already write read-only data blocks to disk, this immutability constraint is not a liability. It represents free memory savings and tighter cache residency across distributed nodes.\n\nDefaulting to classic Bloom filters in read-heavy immutable pipelines leaves valuable memory on the table."
    },
    {
      "title": "Agent teams and formal verification optimize quantum algorithms",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://deepmind.google/blog/finding-faster-quantum-algorithms-with-agents-and-formal-verification/",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "Sergey Shirobokov",
        "Ben Jaderberg",
        "Ryan Babbush"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how collaborating AI agent teams and formal proof systems can design dramatically more efficient quantum circuits. You will learn how automated mathematical verification overcomes the limitations of testing resource-heavy algorithms on current hardware.",
      "hn_id": "50626373",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626373",
      "image": null,
      "content": "DeepMind demonstrated that autonomous agent teams can optimize complex quantum circuits when paired with formal mathematical verification. The key bottleneck in using LLMs for scientific discovery has always been hallucination, especially in domains where classical simulation cannot evaluate correctness.\n\nInstead of trusting raw model generations, DeepMind integrated teams of Gemini agents directly with the Lean theorem prover. Multiple independent agent teams proposed algorithmic variants, which Lean formally verified before passing the solutions into a shared knowledge pool. The teams then met periodically in automated virtual syncs to compare and build upon proven solutions.\n\nThis architecture completely eliminates non-viable candidates at machine speed. By offloading correctness checking to a formal deterministic solver, the probabilistic LLMs focus purely on creative heuristic search.\n\nPairing LLM reasoning engines with deterministic formal verification tools provides a robust blueprint for high-stakes applied AI systems."
    },
    {
      "title": "Compiling WebAssembly to native RISC-V directly on resource-constrained microcontrollers",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/llllluca/wasmc",
      "date": "2026-11-24",
      "authors": [
        "llllluca"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how wasmc enables fast on-device compilation of WebAssembly binaries into native RISC-V machine code within tight microcontroller memory budgets.",
      "hn_id": "50626718",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50626718",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Running WebAssembly on tiny microcontrollers usually forces you into a brutal trade-off. You either accept the performance drag of an interpreter or rely on a powerful host machine to pre-compile your binaries ahead of time.\n\nA project called wasmc changes this equation by running an ahead-of-time compiler directly on the microcontroller itself. It takes standard WebAssembly 1.0 modules and compiles them into native 32-bit RISC-V machine code (RV32IM) right inside devices as small as an ESP32-C3, which has roughly 400 KB of RAM.\n\nThe entire compilation pass runs in tens of milliseconds while consuming only a few kilobytes of heap. Instead of introducing an esoteric runtime format, it directly outputs the AOT format used by the WebAssembly Micro Runtime (WAMR), allowing immediate in-place execution.\n\nCompiling directly on edge hardware removes the need for brittle external build steps while retaining native execution speeds."
    },
    {
