      "content": "Unplanned failover in distributed databases is fundamentally an exercise in risk management under incomplete information. Unlike a graceful switchover where the control plane orchestrates a coordinated handoff, an unexpected outage strips away your ability to query the primary for its final write-ahead log position.\n\nThe immediate engineering challenge is differentiating between a dead primary and a localized network partition. If the control plane simply promotes the most advanced replica after a few dropped health checks, it risks creating a split-brain scenario where two nodes accept writes concurrently.\n\nReliable consensus requires validating reachability from multiple viewpoints across the cluster before triggering an election. Once confirmed, the control plane must inspect replication lag across every healthy replica, select the candidate with the highest log sequence number, and fence off the old primary completely before accepting traffic.\n\nFencing is not optional; assuming an unresponsive node is dead will eventually corrupt your data."
