      "content": "When an autonomous agent executes arbitrary commands on a database, catastrophic failure is usually a matter of time. Most teams assume modern safety guardrails or prompt engineering will protect them. However, empirical benchmarking across nine database engines reveals a different reality: engines differ far less in what an agent can destroy than in what they permit an operator to undo.\n\nIn an exhaustive study spanning 9,468 trials across PostgreSQL, MySQL, ClickHouse, and Cassandra, researchers evaluated how agents interact with latent hazards. The findings demonstrate that relying on external guardrails or model alignment fails to prevent accidental drops or corrupted states. Instead, the final blast radius is determined by engine-native transaction boundaries and fine-grained permission arms.\n\nIf you give an autonomous agent access to operational data stores, do not rely on prompt wrappers or external middleware. Treat the agent as an untrusted actor by enforcing transaction-level rollback guarantees and read-only replicas at the engine layer."
