      "content": "Pinning semantic versions in dependency files does not protect a build against compromised upstream releases. PyPI allows file additions to existing releases for up to 14 days, and mutable Git tags in CI actions can easily point to poisoned commits without changing version strings.\n\nTrue supply-chain hardening requires cryptographic hash-pinning for every transitive artifact. When using modern tooling like uv, lockfiles record SHA-256 hashes for each distributable wheel and source archive. The installer validates that downloaded bytes match the recorded checksum before executing setup scripts or compiling packages.\n\nA similar hygiene rule applies to GitHub Actions. Using full commit SHAs instead of version tags prevents an attacker who compromises an upstream action repository from silently altering CI behavior.\n\nImmutable verification at the binary hash level remains the only reliable defense against compromised maintainer accounts and distribution platforms."
