      "content": "Expanding context windows to one million tokens has created a false sense of security in agent architectures. In autonomous runs exceeding forty tool invocations, raw KV cache dilution triggers severe performance degradation, often causing models to hallucinate or drop instructions.\n\nA critical design rule highlighted in this specification is never to summarize failed tool executions. Compacting error logs into high-level summaries strips out critical stack traces, which directly leads the agent into repetitive failure loops. Raw tracebacks must remain intact until resolution.\n\nAnother practical takeaway centers on output formatting. Relying on prompt engineering or post-hoc JSON validation results in roughly eight percent failure rates in structured workflows. Enforcing context-free grammar constraints directly on logits masks before softmax pushes schema validity above ninety-nine percent while eliminating retry latency.\n\nIsolating agent sandboxes requires equal rigor. Running untrusted generated code demands microVM primitives like Firecracker or gVisor rather than bare Docker containers to avoid escape vectors during tool execution.\n\nRobust autonomous agents depend far more on disciplined context compaction and strict decoding boundaries than raw model scale."
