      "content": "Using large language models to audit AI agent configurations for prompt injections is fundamentally flawed because models can be tricked by the very attacks they are evaluating. Attackers are now hiding malicious instructions in MCP servers and skill markdown files through multi-layer encoding and visual cloaking.\n\nDecloak solves this supply chain risk with a completely deterministic, offline scanner written in Go. The binary contains zero networking or execution dependencies, preventing any telemetry leaks during evaluation. It parses skill files using tree-sitter taint tracking across code fences and unwraps four layers of nested Base64, hex, and gzip payloads.\n\nIn benchmark tests on MaliciousSkillBench, this static approach achieved 36.7 percent recall with only 3.3 percent false positives, outperforming LLM-based scanners. It also provides hash pinning to prevent silent upstream configuration drift in continuous integration pipelines.\n\nDeterministic static analysis remains the most dependable foundation for securing AI agent infrastructure."
